.TH TOKENSHARE 1 "@DATE@" "tokenshare @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tokenshare \- route ollama requests among several machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tokenshare
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR PORT ]
.RB [ \-\-fc
.IR URL ]
.RB [ \-\-advertise\-url
.IR URL ]
.RB [ \-\-ollama\-host
.IR URL ]...
.RB [ \-\-client\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-masquerade ]
.RB [ \-\-ollama\-port
.IR PORT ]
.RB [ \-\-pool\-size
.IR N ]
.br
.B tokenshare
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR PORT ]
.B delegates
.br
.B tokenshare
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR PORT ]
.B forget
.I TARGET
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tokenshare
runs a delegate: a small server that listens on one port (11343 by default) and
speaks the ollama API.
A request for a model is served by the local ollama when it has the model and a
free connection, and is otherwise streamed from another delegate that has it.
Delegates find each other by joining through a first-contact delegate and then
exchange keepalives and delegate tables.
.PP
Point an ollama client at a delegate instead of at ollama:
.PP
.RS
.nf
OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11343 ollama run llama3.2
.fi
.RE
.PP
There is no authentication or encryption.
Run it only on a trusted network such as a LAN or a VPN, and never expose the
port to the internet.
.PP
Without a configuration file the delegate uses the ollama at
.I http://localhost:11434
and listens on port 11343.
.SH OPTIONS
Options override the configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read configuration from
.I FILE
instead of the per-user default (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Listen on
.IR PORT .
Ollama requests and coordination traffic share this port.
.TP
.BI \-\-fc " URL"
First-contact delegate to join at startup, for example
.IR http://192.168.2.3:11343 .
It is an ordinary delegate.
.TP
.BI \-\-advertise\-url " URL"
The address other delegates should use to reach this one.
By default it is learned from how the first-contact delegate was reached.
Set it when the machine has several addresses or sits behind NAT.
.TP
.BI \-\-ollama\-host " URL"
An ollama instance reachable from this machine.
May be given several times.
.TP
.B \-\-client\-only
Use no local ollama and borrow only other delegates' models.
Overrides
.BR \-\-ollama\-host .
.TP
.B \-\-masquerade
Also listen on the ollama port (11434 unless
.B \-\-ollama\-port
is given), so applications already pointed at ollama use the pool unchanged.
The tokenshare port stays open for other delegates: requests they forward to it
are served from the ollama hosts while sharing is on, but applications that
connect to it directly get a delegate with no models, which forwards to other
delegates.
Move the local ollama to another port first, for example
.BR "OLLAMA_HOST=127.0.0.1:11435" ,
and list that address as the ollama host.
The delegate refuses to start if an ollama host points back at itself or if a
port is in use.
.TP
.BI \-\-ollama\-port " PORT"
Port for ollama clients when masquerading.
Default 11434, ollama's own.
Choose another to leave a local ollama on 11434.
.TP
.BI \-\-pool\-size " N"
Maximum number of concurrent requests sent to the local ollama (default 3).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is TOML.
Every key is optional.
.TP
.B port
Listen port.
Default 11343.
.TP
.B masquerade
Also listen on
.BR ollama_port ,
for ollama clients.
.B port
then serves other delegates (and clients get no local models there).
Default false.
.TP
.B ollama_port
The port ollama clients use while masquerading.
Default 11434, ollama's own.
.TP
.B fc
First-contact delegate URL.
Default none.
.TP
.B advertise_url
How other delegates reach this one.
Default: detected.
.TP
.B ollama_hosts
List of ollama URLs.
Default
.IR [\(dqhttp://localhost:11434\(dq] .
An empty list makes a client-only delegate.
.TP
.B pool_size
Concurrent requests to ollama.
Default 3.
.TP
.B share_with_peers
When false, other delegates see this machine as serving nothing and their
forwarded requests are refused, while local use is unchanged.
Applies the same
while
.B masquerade
is on.
Default true.
.TP
.B poll_interval_secs
How often to refresh the local model list.
Default 10.
.TP
.B rejoin_retry_secs
Wait before retrying when no delegate could be reached at startup.
Default 300.
.TP
.B keepalive_interval_secs
How often to send a keepalive to one random delegate.
Default 30.
.TP
.B keepalive_ttl
Extra hops a keepalive is relayed.
Default 3.
.TP
.B sync_interval_secs
How often to pull the full delegate table from one random delegate.
Default 60.
.TP
.B shutdown_grace_secs
On shutdown, how long to let requests in flight finish before cutting them off.
Default 30.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fc = "http://192.168.2.3:11343"
ollama_hosts = ["http://localhost:11434"]
pool_size = 3
.fi
.RE
.SH ROUTING
A request that names a model is served locally if the local ollama has the model
and a connection is free.
Otherwise it is sent to an online delegate that has the model, chosen at random,
trying the next if one is busy, unreachable or returns an error.
If all are busy the reply is 503 with a Retry-After header; if no delegate has
the model it is 404.
.PP
Responses are streamed as they arrive.
A request is retried on another delegate only before the first response byte;
if a stream fails midway the client connection is broken rather than ended
cleanly.
.PP
.B GET /api/tags
lists the models of the whole pool.
Other ollama endpoints are answered by the local ollama only.
.SH STATUS ENDPOINT
.B GET /_ts/status
returns JSON describing the delegate: its
.IR id ,
.IR state ,
.IR draining ,
.I sharing
(whether other delegates may use this machine's ollama),
.I ports
(every port it listens on),
.IR pool ,
the local
.I ollama
hosts and the known
.IR delegates .
The
.I state
is one of:
.TP
.B active
a model is available and nothing is running;
.TP
.B in-use
requests are in flight;
.TP
.B no-models
no model is available anywhere, but other delegates are online;
.TP
.B down
no model is available and no other delegate is online.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log level, for example
.BR debug .
Default
.BR info .
Logs record request metrics (path, model, status, timing) and never request
content.
.SH COMMANDS
These talk to the delegate already running on this machine (on
.B \-\-port
or the configured port).
They only work from this machine: the delegate refuses them from the network.
.TP
.B delegates
List the delegates this machine knows about, with their status, when each was
last heard from, their address and their models.
.TP
.BI forget " TARGET"
Forget a delegate, for example one that is gone for good.
.I TARGET
is its id, the start of its id (at least 4 characters), or its URL.
It stays out of the table until it is heard from again with a newer timestamp,
so forgetting a dead delegate sticks and forgetting a live one lasts only until
its next keepalive.
This delegate cannot forget itself.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tokenshare/config.toml
Configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tokenshare/id
This delegate's random ID.
Do not copy it to another machine.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tokenshare/table.json
Saved list of other delegates, used to rejoin after a restart.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/tokenshare.service
systemd user unit.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/tokenshare/
README and an example configuration.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
shut the delegate down gracefully: it stops gossiping, refuses new model
requests with 503, waits up to
.B shutdown_grace_secs
for requests in flight, then cuts off any still streaming.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run as a systemd user service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now tokenshare
journalctl \-\-user \-u tokenshare \-f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Join an existing pool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tokenshare \-\-fc http://192.168.2.3:11343
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what the delegate sees:
.PP
.RS
.nf
curl \-s http://localhost:11343/_ts/status
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ollama (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/tokenshare/README.md
